<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Bavaria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> is the southern and south-eastern part of Bavaria, around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> and the Alpine foothills. It emerged as a separate duchy in the Wittelsbach partition of 1255, when Louis II the Strict received it with Munich as his residence, and the duchies of Bavaria-Munich and Bavaria-Ingolstadt were carved from it in 1392. Reunited from 1505, it is today an administrative region of the German state of Bavaria.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-bavaria.jpg" alt="Upper Bavaria" width="320" height="316" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: TUBS · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Locator_map_RB_Oberbayern_in_Bavaria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> lies in the southern part of Bavaria and is centred on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, the state capital and seat of the district government. It is by far the most populous administrative division in Bavaria. The name refers to the relative position on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and its tributaries: downstream, Upper Bavaria is followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-bavaria">Lower Bavaria</a>, then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-austria">Upper Austria</a>, and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a>. The region has 20 districts and 500 municipalities, including three district-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">free cities</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a>, Munich and Rosenheim, and is divided into four planning regions.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The duchy of Upper Bavaria was first created by the First Bavarian partition in 1255 under Duke Louis II, though it did not correspond exactly to the present territory. After the reunification of 1340 Bavaria was divided again in 1349, and in 1392 the duchies of Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> and Bavaria-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> were created in Upper Bavaria. Bavaria was permanently reunited in 1505. For administration it was split into Rentämter, and Upper Bavaria consisted of the Rentamt Munich and the Rentamt Burghausen.</p><p>After the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> was founded the state was reorganised, and in 1808 it was divided into 15 districts, created in the fashion of the French departments and named after their main rivers. Territorial changes, among them the loss of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and the addition of the Palatinate, reduced them to eight, one of which was the Isarkreis. In 1837 King Ludwig I renamed the districts after historical names and tribes, and the Isarkreis became Upper Bavaria. These districts were predecessors of modern regional self-government, linking the Bavarian state to the local authorities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> is one of the seven administrative regions of Bavaria. Former residence cities include <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neuburg-an-der-donau">Neuburg an der Donau</a>, and the diocesan towns are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freising">Freising</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eichstatt">Eichstätt</a>. The Zugspitze is its highest mountain, and its lakes include the Tegernsee, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-starnberg">Starnberger See</a>, the Ammersee and the Chiemsee. The monasteries of Andechs, Ettal and Benediktbeuern and the Wieskirche hold sacred art, the royal castles of Ludwig II stand at Linderhof and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herrenchiemsee">Herrenchiemsee</a>, and Altötting and Tuntenhausen are the most important places of pilgrimage.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upper Bavaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Duke Louis of <b>Upper Bavaria</b> defeated his cousin Frederick the Fair, the Habsburg duke of Austria, in a dispute over the guardianship of the young dukes of Lower Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/november-9">9 November in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In the Bavarian partition of 1392 Stephen III received the lands around Ingolstadt, scattered across <b>Upper Bavaria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-ingolstadt">Duchy of Bavaria-Ingolstadt</a></li><li><blockquote>The partition of 1392 gave John II the lands around Munich, the smallest share of the Wittelsbach inheritance in <b>Upper Bavaria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-bavaria-munich">Duchy of Bavaria-Munich</a></li><li><blockquote>The duchy was repeatedly divided among co-rulers, and Louis IV of <b>Upper Bavaria</b> acted as guardian of its young dukes, defeating the Austrians at Gammelsdorf in 1313.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-lower-bavaria">Duchy of Lower Bavaria</a></li><li><blockquote>Louis II the Strict received <b>Upper Bavaria</b>, with Munich as residence, together with the Rhenish Palatinate, while his brother Henry XIII took the Duchy of Lower Bavaria.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-upper-bavaria">Duchy of Upper Bavaria</a></li></ul></section>
